 talk  about what's good I'll give you my kind  of  sandwich format I'll give you the good  than the imperfect and then kind of wind  up with the good and maybe a suggestion  on how to use it  so the good things first one good thing  is just that it's an all audio program  and it really gets you familiar with the  phonology of the language the sounds of  the language I'm big on that I really  think it's important at the beginning  when you learn a language to really get  down the phonology I think that's what  that's what helps you think in that  language is what is really getting the  phonology down that's kind of the access  to the inner core of the language the  culture of the language Pimsleur helps  to do that because you're you're  immersed in the the sounds right from  the beginning and you're not relying on  the visual input you're kind of forced  to listen to the sounds and make sense  of them audibly without having that  visual input of reading so it's really  good for that so that's one thing and it  does it in a way that also drills the  sentence structure so you're not just  like listening and repeating two sounds  or phrases or sentences it's actually  building up piece by piece it's it's  really structured in a way that helps  you to build progressively more  difficult sentences as you go another  good thing about Pimsleur is that it  uses something called graduated  intervals which is one of the features  that it's famous for I guess graduated  intervals means that you learn some  language like vocabulary or phrase or  structure then you review it soon after  after a short interval of time but then  the next time you review it the interval  of time gets a little longer and then  the next time you review it there's a  little bit longer between the reviews so  the amount of time between each each  review each time you are asked to recall  that language gets greater and greater  so that's good for for basically  internalizing it into your long-term  memory the more times that you review  that that language the less frequently  you have to review it that's basically  the point if you're familiar with unki  flashcards they use something as a  similar system to that where these space  repetitions where if you get the card  right then the next time you see it is  after a greater interval if you get the  card wrong or you are  confident about it then you see it after  a shorter interval is something similar  to graduated intervals another good  thing is that thumbs ler gets you just  to repeat things a lot like you're  repeating the same structures in the  same both basic vocabulary over and over  and over and when you're first learning  a language it's important to do that to  repeat things over a lot to get some  automaticity to get things internalized  to the point where you can save them  without really thinking especially for  structures like basic sentence  structures that's good because you get  those structures that come out  automatically and then you can  substitute vocabulary and make new  sentences that you've never made before  so it drills a lot of that kind of thing  it gets you repeating a lot but it does  it in a way that always seems like  you're learning something new okay so  onto some of the things that are  imperfect or things that I need I think  need to be supplemented or whatever um I  don't think that the audio only is  always sufficient there are always some  times in a Pimsleur course where I can't  clearly hear what the speaker is saying  on the CDs I think everyone perceives  sounds a little bit differently and  there are sounds that you might be able  to perceive very easily that I hear them  and I can't quite hear what what they're  saying I just don't know what letters  those would come out to if written down  or I just I can't imagine what the word  is I can't really perceive the sounds  and in those kind of situations they  don't really they don't know which  sounds I'm not perceiving